        "text": "Public EV charging in the UK is legally subject to the standard 20% commercial VAT, whereas domestic home electricity benefits from a reduced 5% VAT rate. Furthermore, commercial charge point operators face heavy capital infrastructure expenses, high-voltage National Grid sub-station connection fees, and peak commercial wholesale power tariffs."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How can UK EV drivers keep their annual charging costs low?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "UK EV motorists minimize costs by following the 80/20 charging rule: doing 80% of routine charging at home on an off-peak smart tariff like Octopus Intelligent Go (7p/kWh = ~1.9p/mile) and reserving commercial rapid chargers for only 20% of long-distance motorway driving. This blended approach keeps total annual fueling costs around £400 to £500, compared to £1,600+ for petrol."
