>
  <!-- Lead In Paragraph -->
  <p class="text-lg text-gray-950 font-semibold mb-6 leading-relaxed">
    Public electric vehicle charging in the United Kingdom has expanded at a breathtaking pace, with over 68,000 public chargepoints now dotting motorway service areas, retail parks, and curbside residential streets. Yet for British EV drivers heading out on long journeys, forecourt pricing has created immense confusion: <strong>Why does charging at one motorway service station cost 57p per kWh while another charger 20 miles away demands 92p per kWh?</strong>
  </p>

  <p class="mb-8 leading-relaxed">
    Depending on which network you plug into, the cost of replenishing a standard 64 kWh family electric hatchback (like the MG4 EV or Kia Niro EV) on the public network ranges from <strong>£21 to over £35 per session</strong>. Even more strikingly, relying exclusively on high-speed motorway rapid chargers can make an EV <strong>more expensive to run per mile than an unleaded petrol car</strong>. In this data-backed 2026 investigative guide, the <strong>VoltSavings Research Team</strong> benchmarks the UK's leading public charging networks, compares exact pence-per-mile running costs against forecourt petrol, and reveals how smart motorists combine home off-peak tariffs with selective rapid charging to keep their annual motoring bill hundreds of pounds below petrol.
  </p>

  <!-- Key Takeaways Highlight Box -->
  <div class="my-8 p-6 bg-gradient-to-br from-emerald-50 via-teal-50/40 to-emerald-50 border-2 border-emerald-300 rounded-3xl not-prose shadow-sm">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
      <span class="w-7 h-7 rounded-lg bg-emerald-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-black">
        <i class="fa-solid fa-charging-station"></i>
      </span>
      <h3 class="text-sm font-black uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-950">Key Takeaways: 2026 UK Public Charging Pricing</h3>
    </div>
    <ul class="space-y-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-gray-800 font-medium">
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
        <i class="fa-solid fa-check text-emerald-600 mt-1 shrink-0"></i>
        <span><strong>Cheapest Rapid Network:</strong> <strong>Tesla Superchargers (Open to All)</strong> remain the UK's lowest-cost high-speed network at <strong>57p to 59p/kWh</strong> PAYG (and under 48p/kWh for subscribers or off-peak).</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
        <i class="fa-solid fa-check text-emerald-600 mt-1 shrink-0"></i>
        <span><strong>Priciest Motorway Networks:</strong> <strong>InstaVolt</strong> and <strong>bp pulse (PAYG)</strong> command the highest ad-hoc rates at <strong>87p to 92p/kWh</strong> (~25p–26p/mile).</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
        <i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation text-amber-600 mt-1 shrink-0"></i>
        <span><strong>The Rapid vs Petrol Paradox:</strong> At 79p–92p/kWh, an EV costs <strong>22p to 26p per mile</strong>—significantly higher than a 45 Imperial MPG petrol car at £1.45/L (<strong>14.6p/mile</strong>).</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
        <i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb text-emerald-600 mt-1 shrink-0"></i>
        <span><strong>The 80/20 Blended Strategy:</strong> Pairing 80% home charging on Octopus Intelligent Go (7p/kWh = ~1.9p/mi) with 20% motorway rapid charging keeps your average fuel cost under <strong>5.2p/mile</strong>, saving over £1,100 per year versus petrol.</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- Interactive Quick-Tool Simulator Banner -->
  <div class="my-8 p-6 bg-gray-950 text-white border border-gray-800 rounded-3xl not-prose flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center justify-between gap-5 shadow-xl">
    <div>
      <span class="text-[10px] font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-emerald-400 block mb-1">UK PENCE-PER-MILE CALCULATOR</span>
      <strong class="text-base sm:text-lg font-black text-white block">Compare Public Charger p/kWh Against Your Petrol Forecourt</strong>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-300 font-medium mt-1">Switch our calculator to <strong>🇬🇧 UK (£ / Miles)</strong> mode to input your exact rapid charging tariff, petrol litre price, and vehicle Imperial MPG.</p>
    </div>
    <a 
      href="/#calculator-app" 
      class="px-6 py-3 rounded-2xl bg-emerald-600 hover:bg-emerald-500 text-white text-xs font-black shadow-lg shadow-emerald-600/30 flex-shrink-0 transition-all text-center w-full sm:w-auto"
    >
      <i class="fa-solid fa-calculator mr-1.5"></i> Launch UK Calculator →
    </a>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 1 -->
  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight">
    1. The 2026 UK Public Charging Landscape (Zapmap Price Index Telemetry)
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    According to official charging industry telemetry compiled from thousands of operational UK stations, public charging prices fall into two broad speed and hardware categories:
  </p>

  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8 not-prose">
    <div class="p-5 bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl shadow-xs">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
        <span class="px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-md text-[10px] font-black uppercase bg-blue-100 text-blue-900">Slow / Fast AC</span>
        <span class="text-xs font-bold text-gray-500">Destination &amp; Curbside</span>
      </div>
      <h3 class="text-base font-black text-gray-950 mb-1">3kW to 49kW Charging</h3>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 mb-3 leading-relaxed">
        Commonly found at supermarket car parks, municipal lamp posts, train stations, and council park-and-rides.
      </p>
      <div class="pt-3 border-t border-gray-100 text-xs font-semibold text-gray-800">
        <div>National Average: <span class="text-emerald-700 font-black">54.0p / kWh</span></div>
        <div class="text-[11px] text-gray-500 mt-0.5">Approx. 15.4p per mile at 3.5 mi/kWh efficiency.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="p-5 bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl shadow-xs">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
        <span class="px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-md text-[10px] font-black uppercase bg-amber-100 text-amber-900">Rapid / Ultra-Rapid DC</span>
        <span class="text-xs font-bold text-gray-500">Motorway En-Route</span>
      </div>
      <h3 class="text-base font-black text-gray-950 mb-1">50kW to 350kW High-Speed Hubs</h3>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 mb-3 leading-relaxed">
        Located at motorway service stations (MSA), primary trunk road corridors, and dedicated multi-bay charging plazas.
      </p>
      <div class="pt-3 border-t border-gray-100 text-xs font-semibold text-gray-800">
        <div>National Average: <span class="text-amber-700 font-black">76.0p to 80.0p / kWh</span></div>
        <div class="text-[11px] text-gray-500 mt-0.5">Approx. 21.7p to 22.8p per mile at 3.5 mi/kWh efficiency.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    Why is there such a massive gap between home electricity (the Ofgem domestic price cap sits around 24.5p/kWh, and off-peak smart EV tariffs are just 7p/kWh) and public charging? A primary culprit is the <strong>UK VAT disparity</strong>: domestic energy is taxed at a preferential <strong>5% VAT</strong>, whereas electricity dispensed at commercial public chargers is legally taxed at the full <strong>20% standard VAT</strong> rate. When you add high-voltage National Grid connection standing charges, localized battery storage buffers, and operator hardware leases, public rapid prices inevitably climb.
  </p>

  <!-- Section 2 -->
  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight">
    2. Network-by-Network Price Comparison: Top UK Rapid Networks
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    To help you map out your road trips, we have benchmarked Pay-As-You-Go (PAYG) tariffs across the UK's premier charging networks. To make this comparison practically useful for family car owners, we calculate the exact financial cost of performing a standard <strong>20% to 80% motorway top-up (approx 38.4 kWh replenished)</strong> on a popular 64 kWh electric vehicle (such as an MG4 EV Long Range or Kia Niro EV) delivering a real-world winter/highway efficiency of <strong>3.5 miles per kWh</strong>:
  </p>

  <!-- Comparison Table -->
  <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8 bg-white rounded-2xl border-2 border-gray-200 not-prose shadow-sm">
    <table class="w-full text-xs text-left border-collapse">
      <thead class="bg-gray-100 text-gray-950 font-black uppercase text-[11px] border-b border-gray-200">
        <tr>
          <th class="p-3.5">Charging Network</th>
          <th class="p-3.5">Max Speed</th>
          <th class="p-3.5">PAYG Tariff (2026)</th>
          <th class="p-3.5 text-emerald-800">20%–80% Top-Up (38.4 kWh)</th>
          <th class="p-3.5">Cost Per Mile (3.5 mi/kWh)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 text-gray-700 font-medium">
        <tr class="bg-emerald-50/70">
          <td class="p-3.5 font-bold text-emerald-950">Tesla Supercharger (Open to All EVs)</td>
          <td class="p-3.5">250 kW</td>
          <td class="p-3.5 font-black text-emerald-700">57.0p – 59.0p / kWh</td>
          <td class="p-3.5 font-black text-emerald-700">~£22.27</td>
          <td class="p-3.5 font-black text-emerald-700">16.5p / mile</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="p-3.5 font-bold text-gray-950">Believ (Council &amp; On-Street Hubs)</td>
          <td class="p-3.5">50–150 kW</td>
          <td class="p-3.5">66.0p / kWh</td>
          <td class="p-3.5">£25.34</td>
          <td class="p-3.5 font-bold text-gray-800">18.8p / mile</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="bg-blue-50/30">
          <td class="p-3.5 font-bold text-blue-950">Sainsbury's Smart Charge</td>
          <td class="p-3.5">150 kW</td>
          <td class="p-3.5">72.0p / kWh</td>
          <td class="p-3.5 font-bold text-blue-900">£27.65</td>
          <td class="p-3.5 font-bold text-blue-900">20.5p / mile</td>
        </tr>
